      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "Language -Usage Notes \n1. Notice that verb forms like è (\"it isU or uis itU\n) and arriva (\"you are arrivingU \nor \"are you arriving\") may translate as statements or as questions. \n2. Time-telling expressions are: \nChe ora è? \nÈ l'una. \nSono le ( + plural number). \nÈ mezzogiorno. \nÈ mezzanotte. What time is it? \nIt's one o' clock. \nIt's (+ plural number) o'clock. \nIt's noon. \nIt's midnight. \n3. Minutes following the hour are expressed by e + number of minutes. Thus: \nSono le sei e venti. \nSono le sei e un quarto. \nSono le sei e mezzo. \nSono le sei e tre quarti. It's twenty past six. \nIt's a quarter past six. \nIt's half past six. \nIt's six forty-five. \n4. Minutes (up to 20) before the hour are expressed by meno + number of \nminutes. Thus: \nSono le sei meno venti. \nSono le sei meno un quarto. It's twenty minutes to six. \nIt's a quarter to six. \n5. Official time (e. g., as in train or piane schedules) is expressed in terms of 24 \nhours. Thus: \nSono le sei. \nSono le diciotto. \n1\\/ -su~ /t's 6:00 a.m. \nIt's 6:00 p.m. \nHosted for free on livelingua.com"
    }
